{
  "$comment": "SHARED ACROSS TWO REPOSITORIES. Byte-identical copies live in optioniaWooCommercePlugin/tests/fixtures/shared/ and optioniaWooCommerceBackend/test/fixtures/shared/. Both are gated on this file's SHA-256 and on rule_case_count, so an edit to one fails both builds until the other matches. EVERY CASE DECLARES expect_passes. A fixture asserting only the final state would repeat 16b's lesson, where the pricing fixture proved both ends and neither language proved the middle: for rules the interesting failures are cascade depth and cap behaviour, and neither is visible in a final state. Every rule carries the FULL published shape, `sort_order` included — the fixture is what the PHP evaluator is built against, so a key the real document always carries and this one omits would be a shape only one language has seen. `option-set.serializer.spec.ts` asserts the serializer's key set against this file so the two cannot drift. AN UNREADABLE CONDITION EVALUATES TO FALSE, NEVER SKIPPED. PHP's first evaluator used `continue`, so a rule with [valid-and-true, garbage] under `all` fired there and not in TypeScript — a disagreement about whether a field is hidden, which under ADR-051 is a disagreement about money. Skipping also made a rule MORE likely to fire the more corrupt its document was. TWO COERCION RULES ARE ALSO PINNED HERE BECAUSE THE LANGUAGES DISAGREE BY DEFAULT. (1) A boolean operand compares as 'true'/'false' — PHP's own (string) cast gives '1' and '', so a PHP evaluator must not use it. (2) A non-numeric answer never satisfies greater_than or less_than — PHP's (float) cast turns 'abc' into 0.0 and would make `less_than 5` fire. Both were measured, and neither was covered before M17.6. One case reaches the cap and expects a REFUSAL with no partial state (ADR-050), because a truncated pass is a wrong price that looks right. See M17.2, ADR-050 and ADR-052.",
